<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Mesoamerica and the Caribbean</p><h1 class="st-title">Republic of Haiti</h1><p class="st-meta">1859 CE – present · capital Port-au-Prince</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Haiti as a republic since 1859, shaped by an unpayable debt, US occupation, the Duvalier dictatorship and repeated disasters and crises.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fabre-geffrard">Fabre Geffrard</a> overthrew Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/faustin-soulouque">Faustin I</a> in December 1858 and restored the republic in 1859. Geffrard signed a concordat with the Vatican in 1860, which brought French clergy to run churches and schools, and invited African American immigrants.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Frequent revolts, often by peasant armies called cacos, toppled most 19th-century presidents. The indemnity to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and later loans drained revenue. The <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-states">United States</a> occupied <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/first-empire-of-haiti">Haiti</a> from 1915 to 1934, building roads but imposing forced labour and crushing resistance led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne-peralte">Charlemagne Péralte</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francois-duvalier">François Duvalier</a>, elected in 1957, ruled with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tonton-macoute">Tonton Macoute</a> militia; his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-claude-duvalier">Jean-Claude</a> succeeded him in 1971. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coffee-economy">Coffee</a> and peasant farming sustained the economy as forests vanished.</p><h2>To the present</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-claude-duvalier">Jean-Claude Duvalier</a> fled in 1986. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-bertrand-aristide">Jean-Bertrand Aristide</a> was elected in 1990, overthrown in 1991 and removed again in 2004. An earthquake in 2010 killed over 200,000 people. After President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jovenel-moise">Jovenel Moïse</a> was assassinated in 2021, gangs took control of much of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/port-au-prince">Port-au-Prince</a>, and a transitional presidential council took office in 2024.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/republic-of-haiti.jpg" alt="Republic of Haiti" width="320" height="192"><figcaption>Image: (colours and size changes of the now deleted versions) Madden, Vzb83, Denelson83, Chanheigeorge, Zscout370 and Nights... · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Haiti.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1859 CE – present</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/21st-century">21st century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Port-au-Prince</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Fabre Geffrard, François Duvalier, Jean-Bertrand Aristide</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholicism, Protestantism and Vodou</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Haitian Creole and French</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/haiti">Haiti</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Haiti</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1942&amp;focus=republic-of-haiti" aria-label="Republic of Haiti on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2721 1490 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Republic of Haiti lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M3041 1746L3041 1746M3041 1746L2570 1008"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M3041 1746h0M2570 1008h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3041 1746h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3041 1746h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Port-au-Prince. <a href="/atlas?year=1942&amp;focus=republic-of-haiti">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
